<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tarrafal camp</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tarrafal concentration camp</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tarrafal camp was a prison camp on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-verde">Cape Verde</a>, opened in 1936 by Portugal's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a> for political prisoners. Harsh conditions and disease killed dozens of inmates, earning it the name camp of slow death. Closed in 1954, it was reopened in 1961 for nationalists from the African colonies and finally closed in 1974. It is now a museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tarrafal-camp.jpg" alt="Tarrafal camp" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gagum · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarrafal_Concentration_Camp_-_inside.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
